<main id="main">
<section class="content-container">
  <div class="hero prose">
    <h1>A toolset for modern CSS development</h1>
    <p class="lede">Assemble your CSS partials, lint, and minify with one
      command. Build color pallettes easily. Get stylesheets for new projects up
      and running in a flash.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="{{ '/docs/getting-started/' | url }}">Get started</a>
      ·
      <a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}">Docs</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <div class="content-container prose">
    <h2>Streamlined CSS processor</h2>
    <p>Assemble partials using <code>@import</code>, lint, and
      minify. You don't need a heavy preprocessor to write modern CSS any more;
      use modern CSS for variables, color functions, and math. The Argot
      processor gets out of your way and lets you write modern CSS the way you
      want.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">npx argot styles/input.css dist/output.css</code></pre>
    <p><a href="{{ '/docs/processor/' | url }}">Learn more</a> ·
      <a href="{{ '/docs/processor/linting/' | url }}">Lint configuration</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <div class="content-container prose">
    <h2>Common language of design tokens</h2>
    <p>Argot defines a shared vocabulary of CSS custom property names that
      are usable for any web design project: backgrounds, text, borders, type,
      space, motion, and more. Adopting a standardizing meaning of these
      property names streamlines early development and eases context switching
      between projects. It also enables some
      <a href="{{ '/docs/processor/linting/color-contrast/' | url }}">linting rules</a> that
      wouldn't otherwise be possible.
    </p>
    <p><a href="{{ '/docs/tokens/' | url }}">Browse tokens</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <div class="content-container prose">
    <h2>Scaffolding for new projects</h2>
    <p>Run <code>argot scaffold</code> one time to set up stylesheet partials
      for a new project. It builds your color palettes, writes a
      <a href="{{ '/docs/scaffold/semantic-tokens' | url }}">semantic custom properties</a>
      template file, and sets up layers, reset, and other basics every stylesheet
      needs. The resulting CSS is yours to run with and modify however your
      project requires.</p>
    <p><a href="{{ '/docs/scaffold/' | url }}">Scaffold docs</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <div class="content-container prose">
    <h2>Guidance for custom properties</h2>
    <p>Argot recommends (but does not require) a three-tier approach for
      managing your CSS custom properties: Primitive values, semantic values,
      and module-specific values. Optional Argot lint rules help enforce this
      practice in your stylesheet.
    </p>
    <p><a href="{{ '/docs/custom-property-structure/' | url }}">Custom property structure</a></p>
    </div>
</section>
</main>
